# gitleaks false positives, by fingerprint (commit:path:rule:line).
#
# Two curl examples in the health-sync guide used a literal placeholder,
# "asfa_YOUR_TOKEN". gitleaks' curl-auth-header rule scores it on entropy alone
# and cannot tell a placeholder from a real bearer token, so it blocked every
# subsequent commit — the scan covers history, not just the staged diff.
#
# The guide now uses "$ASFA_TOKEN" from the environment, which is better advice
# anyway (a literal token on a command line ends up in shell history). These two
# entries only silence the finding in the commit that already shipped the old
# wording, which cannot be edited without rewriting pushed history.
#
# Nothing here was ever a live credential: the ASFA sync token is stored as a
# SHA-256 hash and is only ever shown once, in the browser, at creation.
